    "linkedin:d78c9e0cda6597ea02185f58": {
      "job": {
        "title": "Software Engineer — Data Migration & Backend Integration (Node.js)",
        "company": "VetDrive",
        "location": "",
        "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/jobs/view/4468629855",
        "platform": "Linkedin",
        "date_posted": "",
        "is_remote": true,
        "description": "About the role\n \n\n\n VetDrive is a practice management platform for veterinary clinics. Every clinic we onboard arrives with its own history — a legacy database of whatever vintage and flavour the previous system used, or a pile of exports, spreadsheets and reports from an accounting package and whatever software they'd been running the practice on. Getting that data in cleanly, and keeping it flowing correctly afterwards, is the job.\n \n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n\n You'll own the pipelines that move clinical and financial data into the platform and between the platform and the accounting systems our customers already use. This isn't greenfield feature work — it's the kind of engineering where a quiet off\\-by\\-one in a mapping table shows up three weeks later as a clinic's month\\-end not reconciling.\n \n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n What you'll do\n \n\n**Data migrations (the core of the role)** \n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n* Own client onboarding migrations end to end: profile the source, build the extract, map it to our schema, run trial loads, reconcile, cut over — with a rollback plan for when a go\\-live doesn't behave.\n* Work with whatever the source turns out to be — SQL Server, MySQL, Mongo, Access, something older, or a set of CSV/Excel exports and printed\\-style reports from an accounting or practice\\-management package.\n* Write reusable Node.js migration tooling rather than one\\-off scripts. Every clinic is different; the shape of the work repeats.\n* Handle the messy realities: inconsistent encodings, dates in four formats, duplicate client records, free\\-text fields that were meant to be enums, and controlled\\-value mappings that need a defensible rule for anything ambiguous.\n* Run several migrations in parallel — keeping timelines, open questions and risks visible to the onboarding team without being chased for status.\n* Produce reconciliation reports the onboarding team can hand to a clinic — counts, unmapped values, rejected records, and why — plus the runbook notes that let someone else pick the migration up.\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n**ETL pipelines** \n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n* Build and maintain scheduled and event\\-driven pipelines in Node.js across relational, document, and file\\-based sources.\n* Design for restartability and idempotency: reruns should be safe and partial failures recoverable.\n* Add the observability that makes a 2 a.m. failure diagnosable — structured logs, run metadata, alerting.\n* Manage batching, streaming, and backpressure so large loads don't take a production database down with them.\n* Test your work: unit and integration tests on the code, and validation checks on the data it produces.\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n**Financial integrations (supporting)** \n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n* Contribute to our QuickBooks Online and Sage integrations: OAuth2 connection and token handling, REST APIs and webhooks, invoice and payment sync, schema translation, multi\\-tenant credential handling, and chasing down sync discrepancies.\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n**Platform** \n\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n* Write and review Node.js services and internal APIs with the rest of the backend team, working through Git and code review.\n* Deploy and support your own work on AWS (EC2, launch templates) via our CI/CD pipelines. You don't need to be an infrastructure specialist.\n* Treat customer data carefully. You'll be handling clinical and financial records for real practices under GDPR — that means minimal copies, no PII in logs or scratch files, and production access used deliberately.\n* Take part in the support rotation, and expect the occasional cutover scheduled outside clinic hours.\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n What we're looking for\n \n* 3–5 years building backend systems in Node.js (JavaScript, with TypeScript in newer code), with real production ownership.\n* Strong SQL. You can read an unfamiliar schema, work out the relationships without documentation, and write queries that hold up on a few million rows.\n* Experience moving data between systems — migrations, ETL, integrations, imports. If you've untangled someone else's legacy database, say so.\n* Comfort working across different database engines and file formats, and no allergy to old or obscure ones.\n* Solid data structures and algorithms. Not whiteboard trivia — the practical version: picking the right structure for a lookup over a few hundred thousand rows, and knowing when you're trading memory for speed or the other way round. A lot of this work is deciding what can be held in memory and what has to stream.\n* Asynchronous Node.js at a working depth: streams, batching, and why loading a 400MB export into an array is a bad idea.\n* Linux and the shell. Most of the day\\-to\\-day happens there — remote servers, log spelunking, piping files around, cron. You should be comfortable without a GUI.\n* Clear written English. This is a remote, async\\-leaning team, and you'll write for colleagues and occasionally for clinic staff who aren't technical.\n* A verification\\-minded temperament. The best engineers in this role reconcile before they declare done.\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n Nice to have\n \n* Docker — building images, and working with containerised services locally and in deployment.\n* Python for scripting and one\\-off data work. Plenty of source\\-side tooling is easier there.\n* Exposure to accounting or ERP APIs (QuickBooks, Sage, Xero), or an understanding of double\\-entry basics.\n* Multi\\-tenant systems and the isolation problems that come with them.\n* Effective AI\\-assisted development. We use Claude and similar tools daily, and we care about people who use them well — good context and prompting, and the judgement to verify output rather than ship it unread.\n* Any veterinary, healthcare, or clinical data exposure.\n\n\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n\n How we work\n \n\n\n Remote, with Git and code review, CI/CD to AWS, and day\\-to\\-day coordination in Slack and Jira. Decisions and migration notes get written down.",
